{"id": 1, "depth": 1, "text": " the 20th century, anarchists have continued to be active in social movements such as the anti-nuclear movement, environmentalism, feminism, and LGBT rights.\nIn recent years, the term \"anarchy\" has been used to describe states lacking a central government, or in which the central government is very weak. This use derives from the work of political scientists who were attempting to classify states on a continuum between anarchy (no government) and a full democracy. The concept was popularized by the American political scientist David D. Friedman, who argued that the state was a form of organization no different than any other, and therefore could not claim a monopoly over the legitimate use of force. He argued that anarchy, understood as a system without a central authority, would result in much greater individual freedom than any government can provide.", "seed_id": 0, "token_count": 166, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-rp11"}
