{"id": 7, "depth": 2, "text": ", clouds, etc. The range of values for these materials varies from 0.1 for a darkened surface to 0.9 for highly reflective surfaces such as glass, with most common natural surfaces having an albedo of between 0.2 and 0.4. Values of albedo are affected by many factors, including viewing geometry, solar illumination direction, surface orientation, temperature, moisture content, and roughness, among others. This makes it difficult to generalize about the effects of one factor on the other. For example, albedos may vary greatly depending upon the amount of shadowing produced by adjacent objects.\n\n## Determination\n\nThe albedo of a surface is often simply calculated using a spectral reflectance curve that represents its optical properties in terms of the amount of light reflected at various wavelengths. However, this method does not account for the variation in the intensity of the sunlight received over the course of a day, nor for seasonal variations in temperature and atmospheric conditions. These two effects can be incorporated into the calculation by taking the average value of the spectral reflectance curve over a time interval, or by averaging the spectral reflectance curves over several days.\n\nAlternatively, the albedo can be determined by comparing the amount of heat energy absorbed by a sample against that", "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 256, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-rp11"}
{"id": 8, "depth": 3, "text": " of an identical object whose surface has been blackened with a carbon soot. The ratio of the energies absorbed gives the albedo. This method is particularly useful when the color of the surface cannot easily be measured.\n\n#### Spectral Reflectance Curve\n\nSpectral reflectance curve (SR) describes how much of incident radiation (energy) is scattered back from a particular surface. SR is a function of wavelength λ and it can be represented as:\n\n${\\displaystyle \\rho (\\lambda )={\\frac {I_{r}(\\lambda )}{I_{i}(\\lambda )}}}$\n\nwhere ρ is spectral reflectance, ${\\displaystyle I_{r}(\\lambda )}$  is radiated flux, and ${\\displaystyle I_{i}(\\lambda )}$  is incident flux.\n\n#### Global Albedo\n\nGlobal albedo is defined as the fraction of the solar energy falling on the planet's surface that is reflected into space.[2] It can be estimated by measuring the total amount of reflected sunlight at the top of the atmosphere above a given area during a specified period of time. For example, global albedos are calculated daily based on observations made at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center.\n\nFor Earth, the global albedo is", "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 256, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-rp11"}
{"id": 9, "depth": 4, "text": " 0.306 (30.6%).[3]\n\nThe albedo value for other planets depends upon their composition, atmospheric properties, and orbital parameters such as distance from the Sun, inclination to its orbit plane, and axial tilt. The following table gives some representative values:\n\n Planet Global albedo Venus 0.67 Mars 0.18 Mercury 0.23 Jupiter 1.0 Saturn 1.0 Uranus 0.74 Neptune 0.7 Pluto 0.57\n\n#### Hemispheric Albedo\n\nHemispheric albedo is a measure of how much sunlight is reflected from one hemisphere in comparison with the other.\n\nFor Earth, the northern hemispheric albedo is 0.62 while the southern hemispheric albedo is 0.32.[4][5]\n\n### Solar zenith angle\n\nMain article: Zenith angle\n\nThe solar zenith angle is an angular measurement between the direction of the sun and the normal of a given point on a celestial body's surface. This angle is commonly used in calculations involving reflection of light or radiation off surfaces. In many cases it is necessary to know this angle so that the actual amount of light or radiation reflected will not vary depending upon the position of the observer relative to the reflecting surface", "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 256, "model": "llama-3.2-3b-rp11"}
